Show that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is an integer $k=k(\varepsilon)$ such that the set of positive integers of the form $p+2^{
u_1}+\cdots+2^{
u_j}$, with $p$ prime, $0\le j\le k$ and $
u_1,\ldots,
u_j\ge0$, has lower asymptotic density at least $1-\varepsilon$.

Let $d_n=p_{n+1}-p_n$. Assuming the prime number theorem in the form $p_n\sim n\log n$, prove:

(a) for every $\varepsilon>0$ there are infinitely many $n$ with $d_n>(1-\varepsilon)\log p_n$ and infinitely many $n$ with $d_n<(1+\varepsilon)\log p_n$;

(b) $\sum_{n\le N} d_n^2\gg N(\log N)^2$ for all large $N$.

Show that every sufficiently large even integer $n$ can be written as
$$n=p_1+p_2+2^{
u_1}+\cdots+2^{
u_k}$$
with $p_1,p_2$ primes, $
u_i\ge1$ integers, and $k\le 13$.

Using the functional equation $\xi(s)=\xi(1-s)$ for $\xi(s)=\tfrac12 s(s-1)\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)$ and the Euler product for $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, show:

(a) $\zeta(s)\ne0$ for $\operatorname{Re}s>1$;

(b) the only zeros of $\zeta$ with $\operatorname{Re}s<0$ are the simple zeros at $s=-2,-4,-6,\ldots$;

(c) the zeros $\rho$ with $0\le\operatorname{Re}\rho\le1$ are symmetric under $\rho\mapsto1-\rho$ and $\rho\mapsto\bar\rho$.
